 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Supply and reset",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The control chip and the LED dice run from the one VDD pin. Power-on and power-off reset circuits are built in; after power-on reset the device waits for data on DI. With no data and all channels off it draws under 1 µA. A 3.3 V supply is supported, and a reversed supply connection does not damage it."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Data protocol",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Data is a single-wire return-to-zero code. Each bit starts high: a 0 is high for T0H (220-380 ns) then low for T0L, a 1 is high for T1H (580 ns-1 µs) then low for T1L, and one bit takes at least 1.25 µs. A low of 280 µs or more ends the frame (RES); a reset time that long means an interrupt in the controller's output does not trigger a false reset, so slow, low-cost microcontrollers can drive it. The data rate reaches 800 Kbps."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cascading",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The first device on the line takes the first 24 bits it receives into its latch and passes every later bit out of DO after its shaping and amplifying stage, so the stream shrinks by 24 bits at each device. Because each device regenerates the signal, distortion does not build up along the chain, and chain length is limited only by the refresh rate wanted: at 30 frames per second at least 1,024 devices can be driven. The next frame starts after a reset low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Brightness and colour",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each of the three colours has 256 brightness levels, 16,777,216 colours in all. A precise internal oscillator (800 kHz typ) and programmable constant-current outputs hold the colour consistent; each channel runs at 12 mA. The outputs are scanned at 2 kHz, fast enough not to flicker on a high-definition camera."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Decoupling",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Each device's VDD pin gets a filter capacitor to ground, typically 100 nF."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Moisture and baking",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The parts ship in foil moisture-barrier bags with desiccant; the bag protects them for about 1 to 2 months. The part is MSL 5a. Before SMT, bake whole reels at 70-75 °C for 24 hours or more; loose parts left in air (surplus, machine rejects, repair stock) are baked at 120 °C for 4 hours. From leaving the oven to the end of all hot processes (every reflow pass, dipping, wave soldering, rework) must take no more than 24 hours at T < 30 °C and RH < 60 %. Parts should be placed on the pasted board soon after printing, preferably within 1 hour. Moisture taken up before reflow expands in the reflow heat and can part the adhesive from the substrate, damaging the optics."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning and handling",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The top is a modified epoxy encapsulant that attracts dust: keep the work area clean, use parts soon after opening and store finished assemblies covered. Solvents such as triamine or acetone can dissolve the surface; isopropyl alcohol is acceptable once tested on the part. Ultrasonic cleaning is to be avoided, or trialled first, checking ultrasonic power, drying and the assembly conditions. Pick the part up by its sides, do not press the soft top with fingers or sharp metal, do not stack assemblies on one another, and do not use it where the pH is below 7."
  }
 ],
